I measured what /cd does with the new folder's four widening settings, and the measurement turned up a false message. Per the task's rule, I fixed that message and retitled the commit. It's committed as cca85d95; build, the full cargo test, clippy with -D warnings and fmt --check all pass.

What I measured: I ran the built binary in a pty against a small fake provider that asks the agent to run echo hi. The last column is a control run that starts yoyo inside a trusted B, to show each setting can load.

| B's setting | /cd ../B, untrusted | /cd ../B, trusted | Started in B, trusted |
|---|---|---|---|
| hooks.post.bash | not run | not run | runs |
| [permissions] allow = ["echo *"] | not applied (asks first) | not applied (asks first) | applied |
| [mcp_servers.x] | not started | not started | started |
| .yoyo/skills/bskill | not loaded | not loaded | loaded |

The false message: the #869 warning was accurate. The bug was in the message printed just above it when /cd lands in a trusted directory. It said the directory's ".yoyo.toml MCP servers, permissions.allow, shell hooks … are live here", and the next line said the opposite. The table shows none of them load. The one thing that does follow trust after /cd is .yoyo/goal_verify.md, because /goal check reads it from the current directory when it runs.

Changes:
• `src/cli.rs`: both the trust ON and OFF messages now say what actually happens and point to #1040. A new test pins both full strings; it failed before the fix and passes after. The three existing trust-message tests are unchanged and still pass. After rebuilding, I re-ran the trusted /cd and the two lines now agree.
• `src/cd_config_note.rs` (test only): a new test pins the warning's full text for the exact config from the run, and checks that config really contains all three sections.
• `docs/src/configuration/permissions.md` ("After `/cd`"): added the setting / untrusted / trusted / why table. Untrusted directories don't load these by decision: there's no moment to consent, and startup refuses the same four. Trusted directories don't load them because this version can't yet. I also corrected the doc's claim that the warning names skills: it only covers the three .yoyo.toml parts.

Issues:
• Filed #1040 (confirmed OPEN): /cd into a trusted project should load its hooks, allow list, MCP servers and skills without a restart. It carries the table above.
• Posted the table and the fix on #869.

The commit still says "Part of #869" rather than "Fixes". The doc's statement that the launch project's MCP servers stay connected after /cd comes from reading the /cd code in dispatch.rs, which never touches the agent; I didn't measure it. If that gets measured, the next session could close #869.

